# XSH 2.3 Error Numbers
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The general rules on errno and the meaning of each error number. The
# error numbers themselves are tested with the function that reports each
# one; the .cov file points from every entry here to those assertions.

assertion  XSH.2.3-error-numbers.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       With errno set to a non-zero value beforehand, a successful call of
           each of getpid, getcwd, open, read, write, lseek, fstat, stat,
           fcntl, dup, pipe, close, unlink, mkdir, rmdir, opendir,
           readdir (including the call that finds the end), closedir,
           fopen, fgets, fputs, fprintf, fgetc at end-of-file, fclose,
           malloc, free, strtol, strtod, time, clock_gettime, nanosleep,
           sysconf, sigprocmask, setlocale and getenv leaves errno not
           equal to zero.
note       The text forbids setting errno to zero; leaving the value as it
           was, or any other non-zero value, is allowed. A failing call
           sets a non-zero value by definition.
test       t/001-never-zero.c

assertion  XSH.2.3-error-numbers.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       A thread whose errno holds a value, while another thread makes
           failing calls (open of a missing file, close of -1, read of
           -1, strtol overflow) and assigns 0 and other values to its own
           errno, still finds its value unchanged afterwards, and the other
           thread sees the codes its own calls produced.
uses       XBD.errno.h.004
note       XBD.errno.h.004 covers plain stores; this one covers function
           calls by the other thread. The threads are synchronised with a
           mutex; the whole test is bounded by an alarm.
timeout    30
test       t/002-errno-per-thread-calls.c

assertion  XSH.2.3-error-numbers.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       The functions that return an error number as their value return 0
           on success: pthread_mutex_init, pthread_mutex_lock,
           pthread_mutex_unlock, pthread_mutex_destroy, pthread_cond_init,
           pthread_cond_destroy, pthread_rwlock_init, pthread_rwlock_rdlock,
           pthread_rwlock_unlock, pthread_rwlock_destroy, pthread_attr_init,
           pthread_attr_destroy, pthread_key_create, pthread_setspecific,
           pthread_key_delete, pthread_create, pthread_join,
           pthread_once, posix_memalign, pthread_sigmask and
           pthread_kill with signal 0 each return exactly 0. In failure they
           return the error number itself (positive, and not -1):
           pthread_mutex_trylock of a locked error-checking mutex gives EBUSY,
           pthread_attr_setdetachstate with an invalid state gives EINVAL
           (page: may fail), posix_memalign with alignment 3 gives
           EINVAL, an error-checking mutex unlocked by a thread that does not
           own it gives EPERM.
note       The failure return of pthread_attr_setdetachstate is a may-fail
           error; if it returns 0 the test does not count it. The value
           must be positive, and must equal the number the page names where
           it does.
test       t/003-direct-returns.c

assertion  XSH.2.3-error-numbers.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.3
class      required
says       When the link count of a file has reached {LINK_MAX}, a further
           link() to it fails with EMLINK and the count stays at {LINK_MAX}.
note       Applies when pathconf(".", _PC_LINK_MAX) is bounded and at most
           70000; otherwise the test is NOTINUSE.
timeout    120
test       t/004-emlink.c
